<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castelvecchio (Verona)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Castelvecchio Bridge, Ponte Scaligero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castelvecchio is a fortress in Verona built from 1354 by Cangrande II della Scala, together with the fortified Ponte Scaligero across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>. Later a barracks, it was restored in the 20th century and converted by the architect Carlo Scarpa into the Castelvecchio Museum. The bridge, blown up by retreating German troops in 1945, was rebuilt from its original materials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castelvecchio-verona.jpg" alt="Castelvecchio (Verona)" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arne Müseler · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_-_Verona_-_Ponte_Scaligero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
